In January 1610, [[@galileo|Galileo]] pointed his small telescope at Jupiter and saw four tiny "stars" beside it. Night after night, they moved. He realised they were moons going around Jupiter. It was proof that not everything in the sky goes around Earth, an idea that got him into serious trouble.

Today more than 90 moons are known. The four big ones Galileo found are named Io, [[@europa|Europa]], Ganymede and Callisto. Ganymede is bigger than the planet [[@mercury-planet|Mercury]]. Io is covered in erupting [[@volcano|volcanoes]], and icy Europa may hide a [[@space-weird-oceans-moons|salty ocean under its crust]].

{{story:galileo}}

Most jellyfish are only mildly painful, and some, like the common moon jellyfish, barely sting people at all. The most dangerous is the Australian box jellyfish, whose tentacles can be three metres long. Beaches in northern Australia put up warning signs and nets during the box jellyfish season.

In India, a creature often called a "bluebottle", or Portuguese man o' war, sometimes washes up on beaches around [[@mumbai|Mumbai]] and Goa during the monsoon. It isn't a true jellyfish, but a colony of tiny animals living together, and its blue tentacles sting painfully.

{{story:pain}}

In India, the city of Aligarh in Uttar Pradesh has made locks for well over a century. Thousands of small workshops cast, file and assemble padlocks of every size, from tiny brass ones for suitcases to heavy iron ones for shop shutters. Many families have passed the skills down through generations, and Aligarh locks are sold all over India and beyond.

A padlock works on the same principle as a door lock. Some use pins, while others use flat metal plates called [[@lever-lift|levers]] that must each be lifted to the right height.

{{story:passwords}}

Ajanta is a row of about 30 caves cut by hand into a horseshoe-shaped cliff above the Waghora river in Maharashtra. The oldest were carved around the 2nd century BCE. Most of the famous paintings date from the 5th century CE, when the region was ruled by the Vakataka kings.

The caves were Buddhist prayer halls and monasteries, where monks lived, studied and prayed. Their walls tell stories from the life of the [[@buddha|Buddha]] and from the [[@jataka-tales|Jataka tales]], which describe his earlier lives as animals and people. There are princes and dancers, elephants and monkeys, palaces and boats, all painted with a sure, flowing line.

{{story:trade-routes}}

The oldest evidence of dance in Odisha is carved in rock. At the Udayagiri caves near Bhubaneswar, carvings from more than 2,000 years ago show women dancing to music. Centuries later, the walls of temples such as [[@konark|Konark]] were covered with sculptures of dancers and musicians.

Today's Odissi dancers study these carvings closely. Some poses on stage are almost exact copies of figures cut in stone hundreds of years earlier.

{{story:odisha}}

Some people keep a notebook. Others use a spreadsheet or an app on their phone. A very old method is the envelope system. You put cash for each kind of spending into its own envelope, labelled "food", "travel" or "fun". When an envelope is empty, that spending stops until next month.

Savings work best when they come first. Many people move money into savings as soon as they are paid, before they can spend it. Money kept in a bank can also earn [[@money-maths|interest]], so savings grow slowly over time.

{{story:taxes}}

A surfboard floats because it is light and wide. To catch a wave, a surfer has to be moving at nearly the wave's speed. Paddling builds that speed. Then the steep face of the wave tilts the board downhill, and the surfer starts sliding.

Good surfers don't go straight down. They angle across the face, staying in the "pocket" just ahead of the curling lip. On big hollow waves, they can ride inside the curl itself, called the barrel or tube, before shooting out of the end.

{{story:waves-how}}

Around 570 CE, a Persian doctor named Burzoe translated the Panchatantra into Middle Persian. About two centuries later, a writer named Ibn al-Muqaffa turned it into Arabic as *Kalila wa Dimna*. From there it was translated into Hebrew, Latin, Spanish, German and many more languages.

In 1678, the French writer Jean de La Fontaine mentioned an Indian sage, "Pilpay", as a source for some of his fables. The scholar Franklin Edgerton counted over 200 versions of the Panchatantra in more than 50 languages.

{{story:panchatantra-tales}}

The first Women's World Cup was held in England in 1973, two years before the men's. It was organised by the England captain Rachael Heyhoe Flint with support from a businessman, Jack Hayward. England won. By then women had been playing international cricket for almost 40 years: the first women's Test, between Australia and England, was played in Brisbane in December 1934.

Australia has since become the most successful team, winning the 50-over World Cup seven times. Women's Test matches are still played, but rarely, and most international games are one-day or T20 matches.

{{story:equality}}

In 1609, Galileo heard that a Dutch [[@eyesight-glasses|spectacle-maker]] had made a tube that made distant things look closer. He worked out how it must be built and ground his own lenses. Soon he had a telescope that magnified about 20 times. To learn [[@telescopes-how|how telescopes work]], he had to experiment for himself.

Pointing it upwards, he saw that the Moon was not smooth but covered in mountains and craters. In January 1610 he spotted four [[@moons-of-jupiter|moons circling Jupiter]]. He later saw that Venus goes through phases like our Moon, which only makes sense if Venus goes round the Sun. He rushed his findings into a short book, The Starry Messenger.

{{story:renaissance}}

ISRO's workhorse is the PSLV, the Polar Satellite Launch Vehicle. It has four stages that alternate: [[@rocket-fuel|solid fuel]], then liquid, then solid, then liquid. Solid fuel is like a giant firework packed with a rubbery mix that burns fiercely. Liquid engines can be throttled and switched off, which helps place a satellite precisely.

For heavier loads, ISRO uses the GSLV and the LVM3. Their top stages are cryogenic, burning [[@hydrogen-fuel|liquid hydrogen]] and liquid oxygen chilled to below minus 180°C. India spent years developing this hard technology on its own. In July 2023, an LVM3 launched [[@chandrayaan|Chandrayaan-3]], which landed near the Moon's south pole the next month.

{{story:chandrayaan}}

The text is a decree from 196 BCE, issued by priests to honour the young king Ptolemy V. Egypt was then ruled by Greek-speaking kings, so official notices were written in Greek as well as Egyptian.

Scholars read the Greek first. Now they had a translation. But matching it to the hieroglyphs was hard, because nobody knew whether each sign stood for a sound, a word or an idea. It was a puzzle for [[@cipher-codes|code-breakers]] with no key.

{{story:cipher-codes}}

Doctors of the time blamed "hot blood". The city council decided the dancers must dance it out. They cleared a hall and a market space, built a wooden stage, and paid musicians and strong dancers to keep the sufferers moving.

It didn't work. More people joined. Some reports say up to 400 people danced at some point that summer, although historians treat that number with caution. The council then changed its mind. It banned public music and dancing and sent the dancers to a shrine of St Vitus in the hills near Saverne. By September, the outbreak had faded. At the shrine, the dancers were given small crosses and red shoes, and led around the altar. Whether the ritual helped or the panic simply burned itself out, nobody can say.

{{story:yawns}}

Rajput kings built their forts on hilltops and ridges. [[@chittorgarh|Chittorgarh]], in Mewar, covers a plateau about 280 hectares in area. Kumbhalgarh, built by Rana Kumbha in the 1400s, has walls that wind over the hills for about 36 kilometres, among the longest fort walls anywhere after the [[@great-wall|Great Wall of China]].

Chittorgarh was besieged three times, by Alauddin Khilji in 1303, by Bahadur Shah of Gujarat in 1535 and by Akbar in 1568. A famous story says Khilji attacked because he wanted Rani Padmini, the queen of Chittor. It comes from a poem called *Padmavat* written in 1540, more than two centuries later, and many historians doubt that Padmini was a real person.

{{story:rajasthan-forts}}

Around 321 BCE, Chandragupta Maurya, helped by his clever adviser [[@chanakya|Chanakya]], seized power in Magadha. He and his successors built an empire that stretched from Afghanistan to Karnataka. A Greek visitor called Megasthenes described Pataliputra as a huge city surrounded by a wooden wall with hundreds of towers and a deep moat.

Chandragupta's grandson [[@ashoka|Ashoka]] carved messages about kindness and fair rule on rocks and pillars across India. One of his pillar tops, the lion capital from Sarnath, is now India's national emblem.

{{story:aryabhata}}

Every December the city hosts the Music Season, one of the biggest festivals of classical music anywhere. Hundreds of concerts of [[@carnatic-hindustani|Carnatic music]] and dance take place in halls called sabhas, many at low prices. The Madras Music Academy has organised a December season since 1927. Fans hurry between concerts, often stopping for filter [[@coffee|coffee]] and a hot [[@dosa-idli|dosa]] in the canteens.

Chennai is also home to the Tamil film industry, sometimes called Kollywood after the neighbourhood of Kodambakkam. [[@cinema-south|South Indian cinema]] now has fans across the world.

{{story:tamil-ancient}}

In 1864, the US Army forced thousands of Navajo from their homeland. They were made to march hundreds of kilometres to a camp called Bosque Redondo in New Mexico, a journey remembered as the Long Walk. Many people died on the way and in the camp, from cold, hunger and disease.

In 1868, Navajo leaders signed a treaty with the United States that let them return home. Very few of the [[@native-nations|Native nations]] forced off their land were ever allowed to go back. The Navajo were, and their reservation grew larger in the years that followed.

{{story:code-talkers}}

In the Rigveda, the god Indra defeats Vritra, a giant serpent who has trapped the world's waters, and rivers flow again. Indian stories are also full of nagas, serpent beings who guard treasure and water, and of [[@myths-garuda|Garuda]], the eagle who is their enemy.

In ancient Mexico, people worshipped Quetzalcoatl, the feathered serpent, a snake covered in the green feathers of the quetzal bird. Across the Pacific, Japan has its own water dragons. Over and over, dragons are linked with water, rain and rivers.

{{story:lizards}}

Some ruins look as if giants built them. At Mycenae in Greece, the walls are made of boulders weighing several tonnes each. Later Greeks couldn't believe ordinary people had lifted them, so they said the Cyclopes did it. Builders still call this style "Cyclopean masonry".

In Ireland, legend says the giant Finn McCool built the Giant's Causeway as a stepping-stone road across the sea. The rock columns are real, and the true story of how lava made them is just as strange.

{{story:earth-weird-giants-causeway}}

Music has always been a slang factory. [[@jazz|Jazz]] musicians in the USA in the 1930s and 1940s spread words like "cool", meaning calm and stylish, and "hip", meaning in the know. [[@hip-hop|Hip-hop]], born in New York in the 1970s, gave the world "diss", "bling" and many more.

India has its own slang hubs. Mumbai's street language, often called Bambaiyya, mixes Hindi, Marathi, Gujarati and English. Hindi films carried words like *bindaas* (carefree) and *jhakaas* (fantastic) across the country. College students everywhere invent their own words for exams, canteens and strict teachers.

{{story:social-media}}

Today the biggest lentil grower is Canada, mostly on the wide, flat prairies of Saskatchewan. Canadian farmers only began growing lentils seriously in the 1970s. The crop handles cold winters and dry summers well, and it needs little fertiliser.

India grows a lot of lentils too, mostly in Madhya Pradesh and Uttar Pradesh. But it eats even more, so it buys large amounts from Canada and Australia. When India changes its import rules, lentil farmers in Saskatchewan pay close attention. In the United States, lentils grow in the Palouse region of Washington and Idaho, where the town of Pullman holds a yearly lentil festival.

{{story:farming-began}}
